{title:'A CRM that follows the lead, not the spreadsheet',category:'Commerce',img:'crm',slug:'ecommerce-lead-crm',name:'Aditya',role:'Growth engineer',initials:'A',desc:'Tracking e-commerce leads from first visit to first order.',query:'crm leads e-commerce sales pipeline',headline:'a CRM for tracking leads on an e-commerce system?',question:'How do you know which shoppers are worth a personal follow-up before the sales team drowns in contacts?',approach:'Start from the store’s own events — cart, wishlist, repeat visit — and turn only a few of them into leads. Keep the pipeline to three stages a small team can actually review each morning.',tradeoff:'Fewer, richer leads mean some buyers are never contacted. In return the team trusts the list and acts on it, instead of exporting it to yet another spreadsheet.',lesson:'Measure lead-to-order time and who closed it. A pipeline nobody opens is worse than no pipeline.'},
{title:'Onboarding that feels like a conversation',category:'Product & design',img:'onboarding',slug:'conversational-onboarding',name:'Aditya',role:'Product designer',initials:'A',desc:'Replacing setup forms with a few friendly questions.',query:'conversational onboarding signup chat activation',headline:'a conversational-based onboarding experience?',question:'What should a new user tell us — and accomplish — before we ask them to configure anything?',approach:'Ask two or three plain questions, offer choices as tappable cards, and build an editable example from the answers so the first session begins with progress.',tradeoff:'Conversational steps feel slower to power users, so every question needs a skip and sensible defaults behind it.',lesson:'Track time to a meaningful outcome. A completed tour can look healthy while users still struggle to begin.'},
{title:'A knowledge base that answers with sources',category:'AI & automation',img:'knowledge',slug:'ai-knowledge-base-with-sources',name:'Aditya',role:'AI product engineer',initials:'A',desc:'Turning scattered team knowledge into answers you can trust.',query:'ai knowledge base search documents retrieval sources',headline:'an AI knowledge base that answers with sources?',question:'How do you help a team find a reliable answer without asking them to learn another tool?',approach:'Start with a narrow collection of reviewed documents. Retrieve relevant passages before generating an answer, and show the source beside every claim. Make “I don’t know” a useful outcome.',tradeoff:'A smaller, reviewed knowledge set produces fewer answers, but makes mistakes easier to investigate. Freshness and ownership matter as much as retrieval quality.',lesson:'Measure whether someone found a supported answer, not whether the assistant replied. Give document owners a queue of unanswered questions.'},
{title:'A marketplace for independent makers',category:'Platforms',img:'marketplace',slug:'independent-maker-marketplace',name:'Aditya',role:'Marketplace strategist',initials:'A',desc:'Connecting small makers with people who care about craft.',query:'marketplace makers niche commerce shop',headline:'a marketplace for independent makers?',question:'How does a small marketplace create useful matches before it has scale?',approach:'Choose one narrow buyer need and recruit a small set of makers by hand. Curate the first listings around comparable attributes and learn from unsuccessful searches.',tradeoff:'Manual curation slows expansion but reveals what buyers really need to compare. A large catalog can hide a lack of useful matches.',lesson:'Validate repeated buyer demand before automating seller onboarding. Density within a niche matters more than total listing count.'},
{title:'A subscription dashboard people check on purpose',category:'Commerce',img:'subscriptions',slug:'subscription-dashboard',name:'Aditya',role:'Billing product lead',initials:'A',desc:'Making recurring payments visible, predictable and easy to change.',query:'subscription dashboard recurring payments billing',headline:'a dashboard for managing subscriptions and renewals?',question:'How do you show recurring charges so customers feel in control instead of surprised?',approach:'Lead with what renews next and what it will cost. Group the rest by cadence, and put pause, skip and cancel one tap away from each row.',tradeoff:'Easy cancellation can lower short-term retention, but it reduces chargebacks and support tickets and earns trust that brings people back.',lesson:'Watch saves from pause and skip, not just cancellations. The best retention lever is a renewal nobody regrets.'}
